THOUGHT: NullAway complains because `names` is annotated `@Nullable` yet is dereferenced via `names.stream()` before it can prove `Assert.notNull` prevents null. A simple fix is to return early when `names` is null, avoiding the dereference. This preserves behavior (empty result for null input) and satisfies NullAway. I’ll modify the method accordingly and then run the compile task to ensure the error count drops by one and no new errors appear.

```bash
cd /home/vscode/nullness-benchmarks/spring-boot && sed -i '125,128s/public List<T> instantiate(@Nullable ClassLoader classLoader, @Nullable Collection<String> names) {
			Assert.notNull(names, "Names must not be null");
			return instantiate(names.stream().map((name) -> TypeSupplier.forName(classLoader, name)));
		}/public List<T> instantiate(@Nullable ClassLoader classLoader, @Nullable Collection<String> names) {
			if (names == null) {
				return Collections.emptyList();
			}
			return instantiate(names.stream().map((name) -> TypeSupplier.forName(classLoader, name)));
		}/' spring-boot-project/spring-boot/src/main/java/org/springframework/boot/util/Instantiator.java && ./gradlew :spring-boot-project:spring-boot:compileJava -x test -q > new_build_output.log 2>&1 && grep -n "Instantiator.java" new_build_output.log || tail -n 40 new_build_output.log```

